---
layout: layouts/page.njk
---
{% include "partials/hero.njk" %}

{#
  Region-info shell (architecture.md §3): one shared region-card.njk partial feeds two
  render modes, switched by a width-only CSS breakpoint (768px, see --bp-region-info-wide in
  tokens.css) — never both at once, never recomputed on resize. The infographic grid's natural
  width is 940px but its columns are fr-sized with a max-width cap (region-card.css), so it
  scales down fluidly below that instead of overflowing.
    - Wide (region-infographic): all 6 regions permanently visible around the map, in two
      fixed-position columns connected to the map by static, hand-positioned SVG leader lines.
      Cross-hover (card <-> map region <-> leader line, Phase 18) is pure CSS (:has()), no JS —
      each card/line carries a matching data-slug/data-region-slug for the selectors to key off.
    - Narrow (region-single): the map alone, full width, followed by one static card in normal
      flow. Tap-to-fill-this-card-from-the-map is real behavior deferred to a later phase
      (korea-map.js/region-card.js are still stub init()s) — this card shows Gyeonggi-do as the
      default so the DOM/CSS structure and mock data wiring are provable now.
  Left/right column assignment follows each region's actual west/east position on the map
  (verified against live centroid measurement, not eyeballed — Korea's landmass genuinely
  splits 4-west/2-east, not 3/3: Gyeonggi/Chungcheong/Jeolla/Jeju all sit west of the map's own
  center line, only Gangwon/Gyeongsang sit east), ordered top-to-bottom (N to S) within each
  column — see leader-line coordinates below, which target each region's actual centroid on the
  map (not the map's outer edge) so the lines visually connect to the region itself.
#}
<section id="map" class="region-infographic" aria-label="Region overview">
  <div class="region-infographic__col region-infographic__col--left">
    {% for slug in ["gyeonggi-do", "chungcheong-do", "jeolla-do", "jeju-do"] %}
    {% set region = regions | findBySlug(slug) %}
    {% set cost = costOfLiving | findBySlug(slug) %}
    {% set modifier = "infographic" %}
    {% include "partials/region-card.njk" %}
    {% endfor %}
  </div>

  {% include "partials/korea-map.njk" %}

  {#
    Static color key, always visible beneath the map (not just once a partial region exists) —
    same "authored once, nothing computed at runtime" approach as the infographic itself. Its one
    dynamic piece is the county list for whichever region the calculator just marked partial
    (korea-map.js's setLegendDetail()); that used to be crammed into the region-card itself,
    where it clipped mid-word inside the card's fixed 130px height. Explicitly grid-placed (see
    korea-map.css) to span every column / row 2, so it renders full-width under the map and both
    card columns rather than as a 4th auto-placed column squeezed into the map's own 420px slot.
  #}
  <div class="map-legend" aria-label="Map color key">
    <div class="map-legend__list">
      <p class="map-legend__item"><span class="map-legend__swatch map-legend__swatch--active" aria-hidden="true"></span>Fully eligible to stay</p>
      <p class="map-legend__item"><span class="map-legend__swatch map-legend__swatch--partial" aria-hidden="true"></span>Partially eligible to stay — decline-designated counties only</p>
      <p class="map-legend__item"><span class="map-legend__swatch map-legend__swatch--ineligible" aria-hidden="true"></span>Not eligible to stay</p>
    </div>
    <p class="map-legend__detail" data-map-legend-detail hidden></p>
  </div>

  <div class="region-infographic__col region-infographic__col--right">
    {% for slug in ["gangwon-do", "gyeongsang-do"] %}
    {% set region = regions | findBySlug(slug) %}
    {% set cost = costOfLiving | findBySlug(slug) %}
    {% set modifier = "infographic" %}
    {% include "partials/region-card.njk" %}
    {% endfor %}
  </div>

  {#
    Card columns grew from 210px to 240px (region-card.css — room for the English name on one
    line, ROADMAP feedback). Total grid width grew from 880 to 940 (+30 each side) to match.
    Every coordinate below shifted by that same +30: the two column-edge x1 anchors (210→240,
    670→700) and every map-side x2 — the map's own 420px column didn't change size, but its left
    edge moved from x=230 to x=260, so every x2 (a point *inside* the map) shifts by the same
    +30 the map itself moved. y-values are untouched — card height changed (item elsewhere), not
    the column layout's vertical position.
  #}
  <svg class="region-infographic__lines" viewBox="0 0 940 950" preserveAspectRatio="none" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
    <line data-slug="gyeonggi-do" x1="240" y1="221.2" x2="388.8" y2="193.5" />
    <line data-slug="chungcheong-do" x1="240" y1="390.4" x2="407.9" y2="352.6" />
    <line data-slug="jeolla-do" x1="240" y1="559.6" x2="374.5" y2="587.3" />
    <line data-slug="jeju-do" x1="240" y1="728.8" x2="315.9" y2="911.3" />
    <line data-slug="gangwon-do" x1="700" y1="390.4" x2="519.6" y2="160.2" />
    <line data-slug="gyeongsang-do" x1="700" y1="559.6" x2="559.9" y2="462.3" />
  </svg>
</section>

<section class="region-single" aria-label="Selected region">
  {% set region = regions | findBySlug("gyeonggi-do") %}
  {% set cost = costOfLiving | findBySlug("gyeonggi-do") %}
  {% set modifier = "single" %}
  {% include "partials/region-card.njk" %}
</section>
